{"question":"What does īthār mean as a virtue, and when is it appropriate?","words":["وَيُؤْثِرُونَ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"It is choosing another's benefit over one's own, a high form of generosity even in need.","reasons":"Wāḥidī defines it as favoring and singling out; Qurṭubī traces it to certainty, love and patience, and Ibn ʿAṭiyya calls costly preference the noblest trait. Ibn Kathīr cites the best charity as the effort of one with little; Study Quran links 2:177 and 76:8. Giving one's life is an even higher form: Qurṭubī and Bursevī cite Abū Ṭalḥa shielding the Prophet at Uḥud and the sacrifice at Yarmūk.","prefer":[],"against":[],"rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v21p380#2/r3","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p287#2/r9","QURTUBI-FULL:59:9#6/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:59:9#7/r5","QURTUBI-FULL:59:9#7/r6","BURSEVI:v9p351#2/r9","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p70#2/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p70#2/r3","STUDYQURAN:59:9/r3","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:59:6/r24"]},{"position":"An inward virtue of placing God and others ahead of ego without distinctions.","reasons":"Abū Ḥafṣ includes worldly and religious interests; Sulamī's other authorities place every person's rights first and see owned goods as trust. Qushayrī says one may even prefer someone already full and that distinguishing people weakens altruism. Qāshānī calls it restraint from ego's share after tasting universal desires.","prefer":[],"against":[],"rows":["SULAMI:v2p318/r4","SULAMI:v2p318/r5","SULAMI:v2p318/r6","SULAMI:v2p318/r7","SULAMI:v2p318/r8","QUSHAYRI:v3p304/r10","QUSHAYRI:v3p304/r12","QUSHAYRI:v3p304/r13","QUSHAYRI:v3p304/r14","QASHANI:59:9/r1","QASHANI:59:9/r2"]},{"position":"Giving everything is excellent for one able to bear want; another should retain enough to avoid begging.","reasons":"Qurṭubī reconciles praise of the Anṣār with the Prophet's rebuke of a man who gave away his entire gold piece then would beg, and cites 2:177.","prefer":[],"against":[],"rows":["QURTUBI-FULL:59:9#7/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:59:9#7/r4"]}],"turns_on":"Whether giving all at personal cost is always superior or depends on one's ability to endure hardship."}
